Synchronization between threads, each over one or two futex words (`sys::futex_wait`,
`futex_wake`): no allocation, no kernel object, constant-initialized, trivially destructible.
Nothing here fails except a timed wait, with `errc::timed_out`, as I/O fails.

| Type | Does |
|---|---|
| `ca::mutex` | lock, try_lock, lock_until/lock_for, unlock; not recursive |
| `ca::condition` | wait/wait_until/wait_for on a held mutex, with or without a predicate; notify_one, notify_all |
| `ca::once` | call(f): f runs once among every caller; a failed `result<void>` lets the next call retry |
| `ca::latch` | count_down, wait, try_wait, arrive_and_wait: a one-shot countdown |
| `ca::semaphore` | acquire, try_acquire, acquire_until/acquire_for, release(n) |

Deadlines are `sys::now(clock::monotonic)` nanoseconds; each `*_for` takes a duration in
nanoseconds instead. A wait may come back without a notify, so `condition` loops on a
predicate when given one.

```c++
#include "ca/sync.hpp"
#include "ca/time.hpp"

static constinit ca::mutex lock;
static constinit ca::condition changed;
static constinit ca::once setup;
static int ready = 0;

auto main(void) -> int {
	if (!setup.call([] { ready = 1; }) || !setup.is_done())
		return 1;
	lock.lock();
	changed.wait(lock, [] { return ready == 1; });
	const bool timed_out = !changed.wait_for(lock, 1000000);
	lock.unlock();
	if (!timed_out)
		return 2;
	ca::semaphore slots{1U};
	if (!slots.try_acquire() || slots.try_acquire())
		return 3;
	slots.release();
	ca::latch done{2U};
	done.count_down(2U);
	return done.try_wait() ? 0 : 4;
}
```

Unlocking a mutex nobody holds traps, as does counting a latch below zero. A thread that traps
while holding a mutex leaves it locked; `ca::locked` (layer 4) unlocks on the way out.
